      <h2>A Brief History of the MP4 Format</h2>
      <p>
        To truly appreciate why MP4 is the format of choice, a little history helps. MP4, formally known as MPEG-4 Part 14, was developed by the Moving Picture Experts Group (MPEG) and standardized in 2003. It was designed as a successor to older formats like AVI and QuickTime's MOV.
      </p>
      <p>
        The genius of MP4 lies in its container nature. It doesn't just hold video; it can encapsulate video streams (like H.264 or H.265), audio streams (like AAC), subtitles, and even metadata. This flexibility, combined with broad hardware and software support, made it the de facto standard for online video distribution. When you search for <strong>youtube to mp4 online</strong>, you're tapping into over two decades of engineering designed for universal compatibility.
      </p>

